21771240234375, -0.00909423828125, -0.0088043212890625, 0.0191650390625, -0.0029544830322265625, 0.0382080078125, 0.0025615692138671875, 0.00848388671875, -0.006076812744140625, 0.0313720703125, 0.0147552490234375, 0.009521484375, 0.016815185546875, -0.018035888671875, -0.0145416259765625, 0.007007598876953125, -0.0025959014892578125, -0.0258026123046875, 0.0178985595703125, 0.01207733154296875, 0.01210784912109375, -0.03173828125, -0.00664520263671875, -0.009063720703125, -0.0009965896606445312, 0.0286865234375, -0.0220794677734375, 0.008575439453125, 0.00464630126953125, -0.0221710205078125, 0.0208740234375, -0.0257110595703125, -0.022613525390625, 0.025177001953125, 0.004337310791015625, 0.016571044921875, 0.01039886474609375, 0.03271484375, 0.034759521484375, -0.0076751708984375, -0.0054473876953125, -0.00592803955078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods\n\nStudy Design and Dataset  \nThis retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024.\n\nMulti-Agent AI Pipeline and Workflow  \nA locally deployed multi-agent AI pipeline was developed for report structuring and quality assurance (QA). The pipeline consisted of three sequential agents, each with distinct and non-overlapping functions. The workflow is as follows:  \n1. The **Sectioning Agent** receives the raw report text and assigns each sentence to an anatomical section.  \n2. The output\u2014a sectioned report\u2014is passed to the **Consistency Agent**, which analyzes the structured content for cross-section inconsistencies, focusing on key clinical findings.  \n3. The **QA Agent** then reviews the sectioned report and any flagged inconsistencies to identify gender-anatomy conflicts and check for documentation of communication regarding critical findings.  \nEach agent\u2019s output serves as the input for the next, ensuring a stepwise refinement and QA process.\n\n| Agent Name         | Primary Input                | Output                                  | Error Types Addressed                          |\n|--------------------|-----------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------|\n| Sectioning Agent   | Raw report text             | Sentences assigned to anatomical sections| Section assignment errors (mismatches, omissions, duplications) |\n| Consistency Agent  | Sectioned report            | List of flagged cross-section inconsistencies | Discrepancies between Findings and Impression (e.g., mismatched laterality, organ, or critical findings) |\n| QA Agent           | Sectioned report, flagged findings | Flags for gender-anatomy conflicts and undocumented critical findings | Gender-anatomy conflicts, missing documentation of critical finding communication |\n\n**Key Term Definitions**  \n- *Key clinical findings*: Clinically significant observations such as \"nodule,\" \"mass,\" \"effusion,\" \"fracture,\" or \"obstruction,\" identified using a curated term list.\n- *Critical findings*: Findings that require urgent communication due to potential for significant patient harm if not acted upon promptly (e.g., new pulmonary embolism, bowel perforation, tension pneumothorax).\n- *Section assignment errors*: Sentences assigned to incorrect, omitted, or duplicated anatomical sections.\n- *Gender-anatomy conflicts*: Inconsistencies between reported anatomy and patient gender (e.g., \"prostate\" in a female patient).\n- *Undocumented critical findings*: Critical findings present in the report without documentation of communication to the referring provider.\n\n**Sectioning Agent**: Assigns sentences to anatomical sections using a combination of rule-based (regular expressions) and large language model (LLM; Llama-2-13B, default parameters, no fine-tuning) methods. Sentences with unambiguous section references are assigned via rules; ambiguous cases are resolved by the LLM using sentence context. For example, the regex `r\"\\b(liver|hepatic)\\b\"` is used to assign sentences mentioning \"liver\" or \"hepatic\" to the Liver section. For ambiguous sentences, the LLM is prompted with:  \n*\"Assign the following sentence to the most appropriate anatomical section (e.g., Liver, Lung, Kidney): '[sentence]'. Respond with only the section name.\"*  \nLLM outputs were audited during development, and systematic errors led to prompt refinement or manual assignment.\n\n**Consistency Agent**: Checks for cross-section consistency of key findings. It identifies key clinical findings using a curated term list (e.g., \"nodule,\" \"mass,\" \"effusion\") and flags discrepancies between Findings and Impression sections, such as mismatches in laterality, organ, or critical findings. For example, if \"left renal mass\" appears in Findings but not in Impression, a discrepancy is flagged. Rules and term lists were refined using a development set of 50 reports.\n\n**QA Agent**: Flags gender-anatomy conflicts and checks for documentation of communication regarding critical findings. Gender-anatomy conflicts are detected via keyword matching (e.g., \"prostate\" in a report for a female patient) and LLM-based contextual analysis. For critical findings, the agent checks for documentation of communication (e.g., presence of phrases like \"results discussed with referring physician\"). LLM outputs were reviewed and prompt rules refined as needed.\n\n**Technical Implementation Details**  \nKey implementation details omitted from the main text and provided in the Supplementary Materials include:  \n- The full list of regular expressions and anatomical section mappings  \n- The complete LLM prompt templates and prompt engineering iterations  \n- The curated term lists for key clinical and critical findings  \n- Details of the LLM auditing and error correction process  \n- Additional examples of ambiguous sentence handling  \nEssential information for reproducibility, such as the main regex patterns, LLM model and parameters, and evaluation rubric, are included in the main document.\n\nEvaluation Subset Selection  \nA subset of 45 reports was selected for independent evaluation by two board-certified radiologists. Stratified random sampling ensured proportional representation of all flagged error types (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, undocumented critical findings) and unflagged reports, based on their prevalence in the full dataset. The subset size was determined to ensure at least 10 examples of each error type, based on their observed prevalence (section mismatches ~13%, gender-anatomy conflicts ~1%, undocumented critical findings ~0.5%), while maintaining feasibility for manual review. No formal power calculation was performed due to the exploratory nature of the study and the rarity of some error types. The sample size was chosen to maximize inclusion of rare errors and provide sufficient examples for qualitative assessment, recognizing that this limits statistical power and generalizability. The primary aim was to enable qualitative and descriptive evaluation of system performance across all error types, rather than to support formal hypothesis testing or precise estimation of performance metrics.\n\nEvaluation Metrics\n\n**Operational Definitions and Reviewer Rubric**  \n- *Correctly restructured*: Each sentence is present and assigned to the appropriate anatomical section, with clinical meaning preserved.  \n- *Incorrectly restructured*: Any of the following:  \n  - Sentence assigned to an incorrect or unrelated section  \n  - Omitted sentence  \n  - Altered meaning (e.g., splitting a sentence so clinical context is lost)  \n  - Unjustified duplication  \n- *Ambiguous/multi-organ findings*: Assignment to either relevant section is acceptable; omission or assignment to an unrelated section is incorrect.\n\n**Reviewer Criteria (Consolidated):**  \n- All sentences must be present and assigned to the correct anatomical section.  \n- No unjustified duplication of sentences across sections.  \n- Clinical meaning must be preserved.  \n- For ambiguous or multi-organ findings, assignment to either or both relevant sections is acceptable if not duplicated or altered.  \n- Any deviation from these criteria constitutes an incorrect restructuring.\n\n**Examples:**  \n- \"There is a right lower lobe nodule\" in the \"Liver\" section: incorrect  \n- \"Mild hepatic steatosis\" in the \"Liver\" section: correct  \n- \"There is a mass at the hepatic-renal interface\": assignment to either \"Liver\" or \"Kidney\" section is acceptable  \n- \"There is a large mass involving both the liver and right kidney\": assignment to both \"Liver\" and \"Kidney\" sections is acceptable if not duplicated or altered\n\n**QA Performance Ratings:**  \n- *Excellent*: All relevant issues correctly flagged, no errors or omissions  \n- *Good*: Minor errors or omissions not affecting clinical interpretation  \n- *Fair*: Errors or omissions with potential to affect clinical interpretation\n\nReviewers received standardized instructions and a training session with example cases. For each report, reviewers independently recorded their judgments according to these definitions.\n\nReviewer Disagreement Handling  \nDisagreements were resolved by consensus discussion. If consensus could not be reached, a third board-certified radiologist adjudicated. Consensus ratings were used in all analyses.\n\nStatistical Analysis  \nReviewer agreement was assessed using Cohen\u2019s kappa statistic. For key proportions (e.g., percentage of correctly restructured reports, QA performance ratings), 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated using the Wilson score interval. For QA flagging, precision, recall, and F1-score were calculated for each error type (section mismatch, gender-anatomy conflict, undocumented critical finding) using reviewer consensus as the reference standard. The primary outcome was the proportion of reports correctly restructured by the system. Secondary outcomes included accuracy of QA flagging, precision, recall, F1-score (both overall and by error type), and inter-rater agreement. Missing or ambiguous cases were excluded from analysis and reported separately. All statistical analyses were performed using Python (v3.10) with the `scikit-learn` and `statsmodels` packages.\n\nBaseline/Comparator Data and Comparative Analysis  \nManual structuring and QA performance without the AI system were not formally measured in this study. However, prior internal audits of manual reporting, conducted in 2022 using a similar rubric (section assignment accuracy, documentation of critical findings, and gender-anatomy consistency), involved random sampling of 100 reports reviewed independently by two radiologists with consensus adjudication. These audits identified sectioning errors in approximately 10\u201315% of reports and QA documentation omissions in 5\u20138%. In comparison, the AI system demonstrated a lower rate of sectioning errors (6.7% incorrect restructuring in the evaluation subset) and a lower rate of missed documentation of critical findings (2.2% in the evaluation subset), suggesting improved performance relative to historical manual reporting. However, direct statistical comparison is limited by differences in sample size and study design.\n\nResults\n\n**System Performance Summary**  \nThe multi-agent AI system processed 638 reports, structuring a total of 22,270 sentences. QA issues were flagged in 90 reports (14.1%) in the full dataset. In the evaluation subset (n=45), 15 reports (33.3%) were"}}